<BaseLayout title="Acupressure for Migraine: Best Points" description="Learn how to use acupressure for migraine relief and prevention. Guide to GB20, LI4, LV3, and other points that reduce migraine frequency and intensity." breadcrumbs={[{ "label": "Home", "href": "/" }, { "label": "Conditions", "href": "/conditions/" }, { "label": "Acupressure for Migraine", "href": "/conditions/acupressure-for-migraine/" }]} datePublished="2025-04-10" dateModified="2026-06-01" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6"><h1 class="mb-4">Acupressure for Migraine: Best Points for Relief and Prevention</h1><p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">Acupressure offers a drug-free approach that many people use to help manage migraine pain and support their usual migraine care. This guide covers the points most commonly used for migraine and a practical routine for during and between attacks — along with an honest picture of the evidence.</p><section id="gb20" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">1. GB20 (Fengchi) - Wind Pool</a></h2><p class="mb-3">GB20 is the most important point for migraine relief in clinical practice. It is used to release tension in the neck and skull base that often accompanies migraines.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> In the hollows at the base of the skull, between the two vertical neck muscles. <a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point/">GB20 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press upward into the skull base depressions with both thumbs simultaneously. Hold for 3-5 minutes.</p></section><section id="li4" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">2. LI4 (Hegu) - Joining Valley</a></h2><p class="mb-3">LI4 is traditionally used for migraine pain, particularly for pain that is frontal or temporal. It is one of the points most commonly tested in headache research. <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/">LI4 guide</a>.</p></section><section id="lv3" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/lv3-taichong-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">3. LV3 (Taichong) - Great Surge</a></h2><p class="mb-3">In traditional Chinese medicine theory, LV3 is especially indicated for migraines linked to stress, anger, or liver Qi stagnation, and is said to help redirect energy downward and calm the mind. This is traditional theory used to guide point selection. <a href="/points/lv3-taichong-acupressure-point/">LV3 guide</a>.</p></section><section id="taiyang" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/taiyang-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">4. Taiyang (Sun Point)</a></h2><p class="mb-3">This extra point is located in the temple region and provides local relief for migraine pain concentrated at the sides of the head.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> In the depression at the temple, about one finger-width behind the midpoint between the outer eyebrow and eye corner.</p></section><section id="gb21" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/gb21-jianjing-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">5. GB21 (Jianjing) - Shoulder Well</a></h2><p class="mb-3">GB21 is traditionally used to release shoulder tension that can accompany migraine attacks.</p></section><section id="routine" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">During an Attack</h2><ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><li>Apply firm pressure to GB20 for 3-5 minutes</li><li>Press Taiyang points at both temples for 2 minutes</li><li>Stimulate LI4 on both hands for 2 minutes each</li><li>Finish with LV3 on both feet for 2 minutes each</li><li>Rest in a dark, quiet room for 20-30 minutes</li></ol></section><section id="prevention" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Preventive Daily Routine</h2><p class="mb-3">For supporting overall migraine management between attacks, many practitioners suggest practicing this routine twice daily:</p><ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><li>GB20 - 2 minutes each side</li><li>LI4 - 1 minute each hand</li><li>LV3 - 1 minute each foot</li><li>GB21 - 1 minute each shoulder</li></ul></section><section id="evidence" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Scientific Evidence</h2><p class="mb-3">Migraine-specific research is the thinnest part of the evidence base on this site, and we want to be straight about that. The best available study relevant to headache is the 2010 randomized trial by Hsieh et al. (Am J Chin Med, <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20128040/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-forest-600 underline decoration-forest-300 underline-offset-2 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300 dark:hover:text-forest-200">PMID 20128040</a>), which found one month of acupressure more effective than a muscle relaxant for chronic headache — a category that can include migraines and tension headaches. Trials in this area commonly use the points GB20, LI4, and Taiyang, which are the points this guide focuses on. Reviews describe the evidence overall as promising but limited in quality, and no reliable percentage reduction in migraine frequency is established.</p><p class="mb-3">Because migraines are a neurological condition that warrants proper medical diagnosis, treat the routines above as something to try alongside, not instead of, your prescribed migraine care.</p></section><hr class="my-8"><div class="bg-stone-100 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-lg p-6 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400"><p><strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> Migraines require proper medical diagnosis. Acupressure is a complementary approach, not a replacement for prescribed treatments. Seek emergency care for sudden, severe headache (thunderclap headache).</p></div></article>
